sysl.regex — what a program uses
================================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
    import sysl.text.is_char_boundary

    /** Matching text against a pattern: compile one, then find, replace or split with it.
     *
     * Compiling is separated from matching because it is the expensive half and a pattern is
     * usually used more than once. Every span a match reports is a **byte offset into the input**
     * and is always on a character boundary, so `s[a..<b]` takes one without a boundary check
     * failing; a group that took no part in the match has no span rather than an empty one.
     *
     * This module is the surface. `parse`, `compile` and `vm` beside it are the engine.
     */

The three files under this one are the engine: a pattern becomes a tree, the tree becomes a program,
the program is run. This file is the part a program calling it has to know about, and it is two
types and seven members.

```
import sysl.regex.regex

main()
    var re = regex("([[:alpha:]]+)@([[:alpha:].]+)").unwrap()

    re.find("write to ed@example.com today") match
        Some(m) -> print(m.text(), m.group(1).unwrap(), m.group(2).unwrap())
        None -> print("no address")
```


A match
-------

What a match reports is spans, and the text is carried alongside so a caller can ask for either. The
spans are **byte offsets into the input**, always on character boundaries, so `s[a..<b]` takes one
without a boundary check failing — which is the reason the engine steps characters rather than bytes.

A group that took no part in the match has no span rather than an empty one at some arbitrary place.
`(a)|(b)` against `"b"` leaves group 1 absent, and `Option` is how that is said: there is a real
difference between a group that matched nothing and a group that matched the empty string, and
`(a*)b` against `"b"` is the second.

    struct Match
        src: string
        slots: []int

        // How many groups this match reports, group 0 -- the whole match -- included. So a pattern
        // with two capture groups answers 3.
        group_count(self) -> usize = self.slots.len / 2

        span(self, i: usize) -> Option[(usize, usize)]
            if i >= self.group_count() then return None

            var lo = self.slots[i * 2]
            var hi = self.slots[i * 2 + 1]

            if lo < 0 || hi < 0 then None else Some((usize(lo), usize(hi)))
        end span

        group(self, i: usize) -> Option[string] = self.span(i) match
            Some(s) -> Some(self.src[s.0..<s.1])
            None -> None

        // The whole match. Group 0 always took part -- there would be no `Match` otherwise -- so
        // this is the one place the `Option` is worth unwrapping on the caller's behalf.
        text(self) -> string = self.group(0).unwrap()

        start(self) -> usize = usize(self.slots[0])

        end(self) -> usize = usize(self.slots[1])
    end Match


A compiled pattern
------------------

Compiling is separated from matching because it is the expensive half and a pattern is usually used
more than once. A program matching in a loop compiles once outside it; one matching a pattern it was
handed compiles each time and pays for it honestly.

    struct Regex
        pattern: string
        prog: Program

        // How many capture groups the pattern has, not counting the whole match.
        group_count(self) -> int = self.prog.groups

        find(self, s: string) -> Option[Match] = self.find_at(s, 0)

The leftmost match at or after a starting position. The position does not shorten the input -- the
anchors still speak about the whole of it -- so walking a text with this gives the same answers as
one pass over it would.

        find_at(self, s: string, from: usize) -> Option[Match] = exec_from(self.prog, s, from) match
            Some(slots) -> Some(Match(s, slots))
            None -> None

        is_match(self, s: string) -> bool = self.find(s).is_some()

Every match, left to right and non-overlapping. The next search starts where the last match ended,
except after an **empty** match, which would otherwise be found again forever at the same place; there
the search resumes one character on. That is the rule every engine settles on, and it is why `a*`
against `"bb"` finds three matches -- before each `b`, and after the last.

        find_all(self, s: string) -> &Buf[Match]
            var out: &Buf[Match] = buf()
            var at: usize = 0
            var src = s.bytes
            var going = true

            while going
                self.find_at(s, at) match
                    None -> going = false
                    Some(m) ->
                        out.push(m)

                        if m.end() > at then
                            at = m.end()
                        else
                            at = next_boundary(src, m.end())

                        if at > src.len then going = false
            out
        end find_all

Every match replaced. The replacement text is not a pattern, but it may name the groups the match
found: `\1` through `\9` stand for those groups, `\0` for the whole match, and `\\` for a backslash.
A group that took no part contributes nothing rather than the two characters that named it.

        replace_all(self, s: string, with: string) -> string
            var out = ""
            var at: usize = 0
            var ms = self.find_all(s)

            for i in 0..<ms.len()
                var m = ms.at(i)

                if m.start() > at then out += s[at..<m.start()]
                out += expand_replacement(m, with)
                at = m.end()

            if at < s.len then out += s[at..<s.len]
            out
        end replace_all

The pieces between the matches. A pattern that matches the empty string splits between every
character, which is the same rule `find_all` follows and the reason both are written in terms of it.

        split(self, s: string) -> &Buf[string]
            var out: &Buf[string] = buf()
            var at: usize = 0
            var ms = self.find_all(s)

            for i in 0..<ms.len()
                var m = ms.at(i)

                out.push(s[at..<m.start()])
                at = m.end()

            out.push(s[at..<s.len])
            out
        end split
    end Regex

    regex(pattern: string) -> Result[Regex, RegexError]
        var p = compile_pattern(pattern)?

        Ok(Regex(pattern, p))
    end regex


The two helpers
---------------

Where the next character starts, for stepping past an empty match. It reads the boundary out of the
bytes rather than decoding, because all it needs to know is where a character begins and UTF-8 says
that in the top two bits of every byte.

    private next_boundary(src: []const u8, at: usize) -> usize
        var k = at + 1

        while k < src.len && !is_char_boundary(src[k])
            k += 1

        k
    end next_boundary

    private expand_replacement(m: Match, with: string) -> string
        var out = ""
        var b = with.bytes
        var i: usize = 0

        while i < b.len
            var c = b[i]

            if c == u8('\\') && i + 1 < b.len then
                var d = b[i + 1]

                if d == u8('\\') then
                    out += "\\"
                elif d >= u8('0') && d <= u8('9') then
                    m.group(usize(d - u8('0'))) match
                        Some(g) -> out += g
                        None -> out += ""
                else
                    out += with[i + 1..<i + 2]

                i += 2
            else
                var stop = next_boundary(b, i)

                out += with[i..<stop]
                i = stop
        out
    end expand_replacement
